1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 O-O 8. c3 d5 9. exd5 Nxd5 10. Nxe5 Nxe5 11. Rxe5 c6 12. d4 Bd6 13. Re1 Qh4 14. g3 Qh3 15. Qd3 Bg4 16. Bxd5 cxd5 17. Be3 Rae8 18. Nd2 f5 $6 19. Qf1 Qh5 20. f4 Re6 $2 21. Qg2 $1 Re4 {Normally Black has K on h8 when he tries this sac, and in reply to a4 goes ...g5.} 22. a4 $1 Qe8 23. Nxe4 fxe4 24. axb5 Qxb5 25. Qf1 Qxb2 26. Rxa6 Rf6 27. Ra8+ Kf7 28. Rc1 g5 29. Qf2 Qxf2+ 30. Kxf2 gxf4 $6 31. Bxf4 Rxf4+ 32. gxf4 Bxf4 33. Rb1 $1 h5 $2 34. Ra6 $1 Bc8 35. Rc6 Bd7 36. Rb7 Ke8 37. Ra6 Bc8 38. Ra8 Kd8 39. Rg7 e3+ 40. Kf3 {Tim Harding personal database. My first loss with the Marshall; not theoretically important. What is a little surprising, perhaps, is that after this loss and a draw in round 2 I finished joint second on 4.5/6 with Jeff Horner and Nigel Holloway in what was a strong weekend tournament, won by John Carleton, whom I didn't meet. In the last round D. Lamb (who won a Â?5 grading prize) drew with 16-year-old Tony Miles who (along with Keith Richardson and R. McFarland) finished half a point behind me. Both Lambs, Basman, Bellin, Peter Clarke and some other strong players were on 3.5/6. An effective Swiss Gambit, made possible by Harry Lamb's blunders against me! In later rounds I had a Marshall and an anti-Marshall with Black and won both.} 1-0

